WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.362
ANY and ALL compare one outer value with an entire set.

00:00:05.362 --> 00:00:09.362
ANY means at least one comparison must succeed.

00:00:09.362 --> 00:00:13.402
ALL means every comparison must succeed.

00:00:13.402 --> 00:00:16.602
PostgreSQL also supports SOME as a synonym for ANY.

00:00:16.602 --> 00:00:23.977
The difference sounds small, but it can produce very different result sets.

00:00:23.977 --> 00:00:25.867
ANY and ALL compare one value with a set.

00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:33.377
ANY needs the comparison to succeed against at least one returned value;

00:00:33.377 --> 00:00:37.642
ALL requires it to succeed against every returned value.

00:00:37.642 --> 00:00:44.880
Empty sets and NULLs can affect the result, so test those cases explicitly when the set is data-driven.
